.SH DESCRIPTION
The daharness \f[B]0.2.0\f[R] deb ships the three rust crates under
\f[CR]/usr/share/daharness/rust/\f[R] as \f[B]source\f[R] \(em
\f[I]scratch\f[R] (port of the scratch store), \f[I]pcap\f[R]
(nano\-pcap codec/dissector/search core with an optional libpcap capture
feature), and \f[I]brain\f[R] (the rust Brain sidecar: transport,
framing, typed dispatch, python tool\-worker IPC, and the OS singleton
dance).
The deb build lane itself stays python + C and network\-isolated by
policy: no cargo Build\-Depends, nothing compiles at package time.
.PP
Building and verifying the rust lane is therefore the user\(cqs opt\-in
upgrade step, and \f[B]daharness\-rust\f[R] is its one command.
The network contract is explicit:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]status\f[R] and \f[B]brain\f[R] never touch the network.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]toolchain\f[R], \f[B]build\f[R], and \f[B]smoke\f[R] are deliberate
fetches \(em crates.io on the first run only: \f[CR]Cargo.lock\f[R]
ships inside every crate and \f[B]\(enlocked\f[R] pins the resolve.
.PP
Installing stays offline; the rust upgrade is explicit.
That is the shape the package policy wants, and why nothing here is
automatic.
.PP
\f[B]Nothing built by this lane replaces the shipped python service
lanes.\f[R] \f[CR]daharness.service\f[R] keeps running the python stack;
tool execution, the registry, the scope gate, and the execution tracker
